---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import line from "../data/line.json";

---
<Base title="Home">
  <h1>The Falco Archive</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    An evidence-first archive of the <strong>Falco</strong> family of <strong>Arienzo</strong> in the
    province of Caserta — and of the Valle Caudina, the province of Benevento, and Brisbane.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    <em>Falco</em> is the Latin for falcon. It became a surname twice over: as a Lombard personal name,
    handed down as a patronymic, and as a nickname for the quick, the sharp-eyed, or the falconer.
    It was therefore coined independently, many times, in many places.
    <strong>There is no single founding Falco family.</strong> The work of this archive is to establish
    which Falcos are actually connected, and to say plainly where the evidence stops.
  </blockquote>

  <div class="hero-plate">
    <img src={u("/plates/pasquale-falco-death-1834.jpg")} alt="Arienzo civil death register 1834, act 69 — the death of Pasquale Falco" />
    <div>
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">The record this archive was looking for</h2>
      <p>For as long as there has been a Falco pedigree in this family, the head of it was a guess.
      On <strong>1 October 1834</strong>, at five in the morning, two neighbours from
      <strong>strada Camellara</strong> walked to the town hall at Arienzo and reported the death of
      the man next door.</p>
      <blockquote style="margin:.9rem 0">…è morto nella sua propria casa <strong>Pasquale Falco</strong>,
      d'anni settantaquattro, <em>marito di <strong>Chiara Rivetti</strong></em>, di professione
      contadino, <em>figlio di <strong>Matteo Falco, defunto</strong>… e di
      <strong>Francesca Crisci, defunta</strong></em>.</blockquote>
      <p>One act names his wife, both his parents, his trade and his street — and confirms, from the
      civil register, what a parish baptism of 1767 had said sixty-seven years earlier.
      <a href={u("/antenati")}>How it was found →</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>What is established</h2>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Nine generations, every one documented</h3>
      <p>From <strong>Matteo Falco</strong> and <strong>Francesca Crisci</strong>, whose children were
      baptised at Arienzo from 1767, down to the present. The head of the line is no longer a guess.
      <a href={u("/direct-line")}>Walk the line →</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Pasquale's baptism, found</h3>
      <p>24 October 1767, in the Collegiate Church of Sant'Andrea: <em>infantem natum ex Mattheo Falco,
      ed Francisca Crisci … qui nominatus est Paschalis</em>. It replaced an ancestor who was never
      there. <a href={u("/method")}>What it displaced →</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Twelve households, read entry by entry</h3>
      <p>Fifty-two register entries reconstruct the Falco families of Arienzo — their children, their
      baptisms, the marriage that needed a papal dispensation, and the children who did not live.
      <a href={u("/households")}>The households →</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>An address, at last</h3>
      <p>Four annual death registers, read act by act, put the family on one street:
      <strong>strada Camellara</strong>. Pasquale, his son Vincenzo, and three more Falco householders
      all lived on it, all <em>contadini</em>, and the neighbours who reported each death came from
      the same few doors. <a href={u("/antenati")}>The street →</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>The bishop next door</h3>
      <p>The church in these registers was the second seat of the bishop of Sant'Agata de' Goti — and
      from 1762 to 1775 that bishop was <strong>Alfonso Maria de' Liguori</strong>, who lived in the
      palace beside it. <a href={u("/liguori")}>What that does and does not mean →</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Why they left Arienzo</h3>
      <p>Forchia, where the family surfaces in 1930, is an <strong>Annecchino</strong> town — and
      Carminantonio married Filomena Annecchino. The move north was a marriage, not a migration.
      <a href={u("/valle-caudina")}>The Valle Caudina →</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>One family, one parish, for two centuries</h3>
      <p>Crisci, Morgillo, Martone, Rivetti, de Lucia, Arricale, Maione — every in-law surname of the
      line appears in the same Arienzo books, over and over. The Crisci marry in three times.
      <a href={u("/arienzo")}>The marriage network →</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Two brothers married two sisters</h3>
      <p>The Brisbane immigration files show Carmine and Raffaele Falco, brothers of Forchia, married
      Francesca and Caterina Zampiello, sisters of Arpaia — and four Zampiello sisters in all reached
      Queensland. <a href={u("/australia")}>The Australian chapter →</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>And a name rarer than the family's own</h3>
      <p><strong>Zampiello</strong>, which marries in at Arpaia in the 1950s, has about
      <strong>123 bearers in the world</strong> — 37 of them in Italy.
      <a href={u("/name")}>The names →</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>The shape of the journey</h2>
  <p>Arienzo, in the province of Caserta, is the home parish — every generation from Pasquale to Raffaele is
  born, married and buried there. In <strong>1913</strong> a son is registered not at Arienzo but at
  <strong>Arpaia</strong>, four kilometres away and across the provincial line into Benevento. From there the
  family moves to <strong>Forchia</strong>, then <strong>Benevento</strong>, and then to
  <strong>Brisbane</strong>. <a href={u("/places")}>The places →</a></p>

  <h2>What is still missing</h2>
  <p>Nine questions this archive cannot answer, and five doors that need a person rather than a
  machine — the two unread Brisbane immigration files, the restricted Caserta scans, Antenati, the
  Museo Alfonsiano at Arienzo, and the DNA matches. All of it, with the searches that have already
  failed, is at <a href={u("/open-questions")}>Open questions</a>.</p>

  <p class="small">Nothing here is taken on trust because it is in a family tree. Where a record has been
  seen, it is cited. Where it has not, the claim is marked. See <a href={u("/method")}>Method</a>.</p>
</Base>
